{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1583749884/14/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"58c02a16\" lines=\"10-26\">\n<description>\nExpand the top-left seed mask into a recursive colored pattern and tile it across the full grid.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- All foreground lies in a top-left rectangle whose last row and last column are a solid secondary color.\n- Removing that secondary-color row and column leaves a seed region containing background plus one primary color.\n\nRule\n- Use the primary-color pixels in the seed as a binary mask.\n- Replace each primary seed cell with a copy of the seed mask in the primary color, and each background seed cell\n  with a copy of the seed mask in the secondary color.\n- Repeat and crop that expanded tile to the original grid shape.\n\nKey insight\nThe solid L-shaped secondary-color border is a legend, not output content; it supplies the color used when the\nseed's background cells are expanded.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"581f7754\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nSlide multi-color sprites to align their marker pixels with matching isolated border anchors.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Multi-color sprites in the interior; each has a minority-color \"marker\" pixel.\n- Isolated border pixels (non-bg with all 4-neighbors bg) act as anchors.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the border anchor sharing its marker color.\n- Top/bottom-edge anchor (non-corner): slide sprite horizontally so marker column = anchor column.\n- Left/right-edge anchor (or corner): slide sprite vertically so marker row = anchor row.\n\nKey insight: The anchor's edge position determines the slide axis — top/bottom means horizontal, left/right (or corner) means vertical.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"46c35fc7\" lines=\"21-34\">\n<description>\nRotate the colors around every 3x3 foreground ring, with corners and side-centers cycling in opposite directions.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- Each 4-connected foreground object is a complete 3x3 ring: eight foreground pixels around a background center.\n\nRule\n- For each ring, move corner colors one step around the four corners.\n- Move side-center colors one step around the four side centers in the opposite direction.\n- Leave the background center and all other background pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- Corners and side centers form two separate four-cycles on the same 3x3 ring.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"bc93ec48\" lines=\"48-63\">\n<description>\nCopy each foreground object anchored in a true grid corner clockwise into the next corner.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Foreground objects are monochromatic 4-connected components.\n- Relevant objects are exactly those whose bounding box touches two adjacent borders at one grid corner.\n\nRule\n- Start from the input unchanged.\n- For each corner object, copy its pixel mask and color to the next corner clockwise: top-left to top-right, top-right to bottom-right, bottom-right to bottom-left, and bottom-left to top-left.\n- Preserve each object's orientation by aligning its bounding box to the destination corner; stamps may overwrite existing pixels at the destination, but source pixels are not cleared.\n\nKey insight\n- Interior and side-only objects are distractors; only components actually anchored to a corner participate in the clockwise perimeter copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"c4d067a0\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nComplete a grid of colored blocks to match a minimap legend in the top-left corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Top-left corner: a small sprite of single colored pixels forming a minimap pattern on a regular lattice.\n- Main area: uniformly colored square blocks (NxN) arranged on a regular grid; some positions are empty (bg).\n\nRule:\n- The legend pixels define a color pattern; each pixel's lattice position maps to a block position in the main grid via a fixed scale factor and offset.\n- Existing colored blocks confirm the scale/offset alignment.\n- For every legend pixel whose corresponding grid position is empty, place a new block of that color.\n\nKey insight: The top-left pixel pattern is a scaled-down minimap of the block layout; the mapping is inferred from blocks already present.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e734a0e8\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nCopy the unique colored sprite tile into every tiled cell marked by an internal separator-colored pixel.\n\nInput structure\n- Full same-color separator rows and columns partition the grid into equal rectangular tiles.\n- One tile contains the only non-background, non-separator colored sprite.\n- Target tiles are otherwise background-colored but contain at least one internal pixel of the separator color.\n\nRule\n- Keep the separator lines and the source sprite tile unchanged.\n- Replace each marked target tile with an exact copy of the source tile.\n- Unmarked background-only tiles remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The internal separator-colored pixels are placement markers; the single colored tile is the template to stamp into those marked cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7acdf6d3\" lines=\"25-42\">\n<description>\nMove the source-color pixels into the unique outline interior whose size matches the source count.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background and exactly two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color forms 8-connected outline objects with row-wise interior cells.\n- The other foreground color supplies a set of source pixels away from the chosen outline.\nRule\n- For each possible outline color, compute each 8-connected outline object's\n  row-span interior: background pixels strictly between the leftmost and\n  rightmost outline pixels on each occupied row.\n- Select the unique outline interior whose cell count equals the total\n  number of pixels of the other foreground color.\n- Erase the source pixels and paint that matched interior with the source color.\nKey insight\n- The colors are roles, not fixed IDs: the source color is identified by\n  counting, and the destination is the outline with a matching interior area.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b0280bc\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nRecolor the shortest path between two terminal squares in a square-and-track graph.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two non-bg colors form solid NxN squares (N>=2): one color marks exactly 2 terminals, the other marks stations.\n- A third non-bg color forms 1-cell-wide track segments connecting pairs of squares (graph edges).\n\nRule:\n- Find the shortest path (fewest edges) between the two terminal squares.\n- Recolor every station square on that path to green.\n- Recolor every track segment along that path to grey.\n- Everything else unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The grid encodes a graph where squares are nodes and tracks are edges; the task is graph shortest-path, not spatial proximity.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e3721c99\" lines=\"69-83\">\n<description>\nRecolor grey shapes by matching their hole count to a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; grey (5) sprites scattered across the grid.\n- Colored legend shapes (non-bg, non-grey, at least 2×2, not touching the outer border) near grid edge,\n  each with a distinct number of enclosed holes.\n\nRule:\n- Each colored legend shape defines a mapping: hole count → color.\n- For every grey sprite, count its holes (enclosed bg regions not touching the sprite's bounding-box border).\n- Recolor the grey sprite to the matching legend color; erase grey distractors with unlisted hole counts.\n\nKey insight: Shape identity is encoded by topology (number of holes), not geometry or size.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8f3a5a89\" lines=\"22-36\">\n<description>\nPaint an inner border around the region reachable from a corner marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; wall color (blue) forms barriers dividing the grid.\n- A unique single pixel (magenta) sits on one corner — the start marker.\n- Some wall-colored pixels are structural (border-touching, enclosing regions); others are decorations.\n\nRule:\n- Flood-fill from the marker corner to find all reachable bg cells, blocked by structural walls.\n- Erase wall-colored decorations hidden behind structural walls (not reachable from marker side).\n- Paint orange (color 7) on every reachable bg cell adjacent to a wall or grid edge — forming a 1-cell inner border.\n\nKey insight: Only border-connected wall components act as real barriers; interior wall fragments are decorations to be removed.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b7955b3c\" lines=\"386-403\">\n<description>\nReplace gray plus-shaped damage masks with the hidden colors of the rectilinear mosaic.\n\nInput structure:\n- Color 8 appears only as isolated 3x3 plus masks.\n- The non-mask pixels are visible parts of a clean grid made from horizontal\n  and vertical color regions.\n\nRule:\n- Treat every color-8 pixel as unknown damage and leave all other pixels fixed.\n- First copy unknown pixels from exact matching parallel rows or columns.\n- Then fill any remaining mask cells from opposite-side continuations and the\n  better-supported local straight edge.\n\nKey insight:\n- The gray pluses are occluders, not objects; the underlying colors are\n  determined by repeated line templates and nearby axis-aligned boundaries.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"342ae2ed\" lines=\"34-49\">\n<description>\nDraw the missing diagonal connector segment between each pair of same-color rectangular objects.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- Every foreground color appears as exactly two separated solid rectangular 4-connected objects.\n- The two rectangles of each color are diagonally offset with facing corners on a 45-degree line.\n\nRule:\n- Keep all existing rectangles unchanged.\n- For each foreground color, connect the lower facing corner of the upper rectangle to the upper facing corner of the lower rectangle.\n- Paint only the intervening background pixels on that diagonal, excluding the two endpoint pixels already in the rectangles.\n\nKey insight:\n- Same-color rectangles define the endpoints; the output completes the open diagonal between their nearest facing corners.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7ec998c9\" lines=\"14-28\">\n<description>\nDraw a blue folded corner-to-corner route through the lone marker's column.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid filled with one background color.\n- Exactly one non-background marker pixel; blue is absent from the input.\n\nRule:\n- Preserve the marker and repaint background pixels in its column blue from top to bottom.\n- If the marker is in the unique center column, connect that column to the top-right and bottom-left corners.\n- Otherwise, connect the column to the top-left and bottom-right corners.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker's column is the route spine; its central vs off-center placement selects which opposite corner pair gets the horizontal edge segments.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3bd292e8\" lines=\"13-29\">\n<description>\nRecolor wall-separated background regions alternately, starting with grey in the lower-left region.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
